{-# OPTIONS --lossy-unification #-}
-- Quotienting a theory by more equations.
--
-- Given a theory `σeq` and a further family `E` of equations over the
-- same signature, `σeq / E` is the theory with that signature and the
-- union of the equations.  The definition is trivial -- it is
-- `_∪Eqns_` from `Theory.Presentation` -- and all the content is in
-- what the construction *is*, which is three things.
--
-- 1. A full subcategory.  Satisfying `E` is a property of a
--    `σeq`-model, not extra structure: `Homo` mentions only the
--    signature, so it does not see the equations at all.  So the
--    property appears as a `PropertyOver` on `MOD σeq`, and
--    `MOD (σeq / E)` is isomorphic -- bijective on objects and fully
--    faithful -- to its total category.  This is the unsorted mirror
--    of the way `EQNSᴰ` layers over `ALGᴰ` in `Theory.Sorted`.
--
-- 2. Half of a factorization system on THEORY.  A map of theories
--    `(F , pF) : (σ , σeq) → (τ , τeq)` splits as
--
--        (σ , σeq) --(F , pushPres)--> (τ , F* σeq)
--                  --(id , pullPres)--> (τ , τeq)
--
--    where `F* σeq = pushEqns F σeq` is τ's signature carrying exactly
--    σeq's equations read through F.  The first map adds operations
--    and imposes nothing new; the second is the identity on the
--    signature, and a map that is the identity on the signature is
--    precisely a quotient map: `quotCatIso` shows `σeq / σeq'` and
--    `σeq'` are isomorphic in THEORY whenever there is a vertical map
--    `σeq → σeq'`, and `addEqnsIsQuot` factors the second map through
--    the literal quotient map of this file.  `orthogonal` supplies the
--    unique diagonal filler, so the two classes are orthogonal.
--    Uniqueness is free -- `PresEqns` is a proposition, so a map of
--    theories is determined by its signature part -- and the content
--    is the construction of the filler's `PresEqns` witness.
--
-- 3. A reflection.  The inclusion `MOD (σeq / E) ↪ MOD σeq` has a left
--    adjoint, and no quotient HIT is needed to build it: the
--    reflection of a model `B` is `B` *presented over the quotient
--    theory by its own multiplication table*, one relation
--    `op(x) = ⟨op⟩⟦x⟧` for every operation and tuple.  Relations are
--    equations with no variables (see `Theory.Presentation`), so the
--    table forces nothing beyond B's own structure, while the base
--    theory `σeq / E` forces `E`; the presented model is therefore B
--    modulo the congruence generated by E.  `UPReflectMOD` is the
--    adjunction isomorphism of hom-sets and `UPReflectNat` its
--    naturality.  This is what makes abelianization an instance.
--
-- Everything is stated for the *unsorted* framework of
-- `Cubical.Algebra.Theory`, because THEORY-as-a-category lives there
-- (`Theory.Theories`) and the factorization is a statement about
-- THEORY.
module Cubical.Algebra.Theory.Constructions.Quotient where

open import Cubical.Foundations.Prelude
open import Cubical.Foundations.Equiv
open import Cubical.Foundations.Function
